course: dictionary
lesson: 4399
title: Hairy: more than hair
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Hairy means covered in a lot of hair. Use the context to check it: «Woolly mammoths, hairy relatives of elephants, lived during the last Ice Age» tells you the mammoths were covered in thick hair, not that they were scary. Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Hairy can also be informal. «A hairy moment» means a frightening or dangerous moment, often in an exciting way. Look at the clues: a close call on a bike or in the car is a scary situation, so hairy means scary here, not covered in hair.
explain: The phrase «things got a bit hairy» means things became dangerous or scary. The same word can have two meanings, so the words around it tell you which one the writer means.
fact: Hairy means covered in a lot of hair.
fact: Informally, «hairy» means frightening or dangerous, often in an exciting way.
fact: «A hairy moment» is a close call, a scary moment.
fact: The forms are hairy, hairier and hairiest: «some caterpillars are hairier than others».
fact: «Some hairy caterpillars have tiny hairs that break off and make skin itch and burn», so look with your eyes only.
exercise: «Woolly mammoths, hairy relatives of elephants, lived during the last Ice Age.» What does hairy mean here? | *Covered in a lot of hair | Frightening and dangerous | Wet and muddy | Very large and heavy
exercise: «That bike ride down the steep hill was a hairy moment.» What does hairy mean here? | *Frightening or dangerous, often in an exciting way | Wet and muddy | Covered in a lot of hair | Very slow and gentle
exercise: Which word means covered in the most hair? | Hairier | *Hairiest | Hairy | Hairiness
test: Things got a bit hairy on the icy road, and the car skidded. What does hairy mean here? | Covered in hair | Cold and wet | *Scary, a close call | Slow and quiet
test: In «the arm looks thin, hairy and smelly», what is hairy describing? | *The arm, which is covered in a lot of hair | The muscle | The cast | The physio
exam: Jayden says hairy only ever means covered in hair. Is he right? | Yes, but only for animals | Yes, hairy only ever means covered in hair | No, it means clean and smooth | *No, informally it can mean frightening or dangerous, as in «a hairy moment»
